      "sequence_position": 6,
      "problem": "Let $a=-39$ and $n=4.$ Find integers $q$ and $r$ such that $a=qn+r,$ where $0\\le r<n,$ and determine $q\\cdot r.$",
      "worked_solution": "Dividing $-39$ by $4$ using integer division, we notice that\n\n$$\n-10<-39÷4<-9.\n$$\n\nThe quotient $q$ is the left-hand value. So $q=-10.$\n\nNext, we find the remainder $r$ by solving $r=a-qn.$ This gives\n\n$$\nr=-39-(-10)(4)=1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write $-39=qn+r$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-39={{(-10)}_{⏟}}_{q}\\cdot 4+{{1}_{⏟}}_{r}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, $q\\cdot r=-10\\cdot 1=-10.$",
